    text: "Describing someone's physical appearance is an essential skill in English. People come in many shapes and sizes. A person may be tall, short, or of average height. Their build can be slim, athletic, stocky, plump, or muscular. We describe faces using adjectives like oval, round, square, or heart-shaped. Eyes can be described by color — blue, brown, green, hazel, or grey — and by shape, such as almond-shaped, round, or narrow. Eyebrows may be thick, thin, arched, or bushy. Noses are described as straight, pointed, flat, or button-shaped. Lips can be thin, full, or bow-shaped. A person's complexion refers to the tone and condition of their skin: fair, olive, dark, tanned, freckled, or pale. Hair is described by color (blonde, brunette, auburn, red, grey, black), by texture (straight, wavy, curly, coarse, fine), and by length (long, short, shoulder-length, cropped). Facial hair includes a beard, mustache, goatee, sideburns, or stubble. Someone can be described as attractive, plain, or striking. A scar, a mole, or a birthmark might be a distinguishing feature. Learning to describe appearance politely and accurately is important in daily conversation, especially when identifying someone or giving a description.",
